resting initiative there are two  really exciting parts of my job the  first one is that I work Cross DG's or I  work across the board of the activities  of the institution so I really have a  good understanding of actually what the  institution is providing and doing and  the second part is that because I'm  working on processes strategies and  systems I I have the impression I hope  it's true but I have the impression that  I can really contribute to change most  of the work is done in English but my  director is French so we interact a lot  in French as well and I'm working on my  Spanish so that's really good and I  would also like to take some non EU  language to expand the scope of where I  can find actually relevant information  for EU institutions the European  institutions encourage multilingualism  which means that people working for the  European institutions are encouraged to  take part in language trainings and  myself I have taken part in several of  them I've started French from scratch  when I came to Luxembourg I had no  knowledge of it before and later on I  started learning Italian and I'm now  finishing that class and I'm thinking of  refreshing my German and Dutch the  language component is so prominent  because of the official languages being  23 whereas in most intergovernmental  organization  you went for example only has six so  that creates a big complexity in terms  of information flows and since I work in  communication  information for us it's an additional  constraint that we always need to factor  in best way to keep one skills  up-to-date is to attend trainings I  think one should never skip on that so  in my current job I do a lot of  communication trainings trainings on  public administration on functioning and  international environment and with  different languages of course I just  came out of a training that was over the  last three days which was the  introduction basically to working for  the EU and I was extremely helpful  because we there were people from  different institutions and it was very  interesting to hear how other newcomers  were experiencing their entry into the  EU environment and in general I think  that trainings really really help in  getting a good grasp of how the EU  institutions work they had the training  in internal communication and that of  course is the the core business of my  unit and I'm going to have a quite soon  our training on video making  I actually have a liking for the adviser  position which I'd like to explore more  and one of the option would be to join  the cabin a of a commissioner which is  kind of the political office of a  commissioner or it could be also to  continue in a more strategic role at  institutional level or also to join a  delegation abroad for example I think  that in these times it's very difficult  to foresee one's future but I'm very  happy and I consider myself lucky to be  working for the institutions and for the  European Court of Auditors  in specific and the European  institutions of a very interesting  career path and if you're if you set  your goals you can also set your vision  to achieve them go for it and if at  first you don't succeed in the in one of  the absurd competitions do not give up  it's a very rewarding career path that  is waiting for you there are a lot of  opportunities for continued learning and  personal development and I'm certainly  very happy and I consider myself lucky  that I've been working in the European  institution so far  you
 

